{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Choppa Docter", "line": "Think I’m Jason, when I’m masked up, nigga", "vault_lines": ["Record that boy like Jason, think I'm Jason, when I'm masked up, nigga"], "meaning": "youngboy is saying he feels like Jason Voorhees from the movie “friday the 13th” he’s saying he feels like a murderer when he has the mask on because jason was a serial killer", "annotation_id": "31663772", "url": "https://genius.com/31663772", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Choppa Docter", "line": "One get killed, he get even, put drums in them choppers I want his life, you can’t save him ’cause I am the doctor", "vault_lines": ["One get killed, he get even, put drums in them choppers", "I want his life, you can't save him 'cause I am the doctor"], "meaning": "Readiness to defend oneself: The lyrics imply that the speaker is always prepared to protect themselves and their territory. They are willing to take action and clean up any threats that come their way.\n\nDetermination to eliminate threats: The phrase “clean a nigga block” suggests a strong determination to eliminate any potential enemies or adversaries in their neighborhood. The speaker is committed to maintaining their dominance and ensuring their safety.\n\nPreparedness to use a gun for protection: The line “they know I stay ridin' with that mop” refers to the speaker carrying a gun for protection. It signifies their willingness to use firearms as a means of defending themselves and maintaining control.", "annotation_id": "28988319", "url": "https://genius.com/28988319", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Choppa Docter", "line": "Hold on, clean a nigga block, they know I stay ridin’ with that mop", "vault_lines": ["Hold on, clean a nigga block, they know I stay ridin' with that mop"], "meaning": "The lyrics suggest that the speaker is ready to defend themselves and their territory.\n\n“Hold on, clean a nigga block” implies the speaker’s determination to eliminate any threats or rivals in their neighborhood.\n\n“They know I stay ridin' with that mop” implies that the speaker is armed and prepared to use a gun (referred to as a “mop”) to protect themselves and their", "annotation_id": "28988313", "url": "https://genius.com/28988313", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Choppa Docter", "line": "Tried to handle me, bitch, no, you didn’t And paper, I been sippin’ silly Hearsin’, I been lurkin’, catch ’em workin’, then I pop out with it", "vault_lines": ["Tried to handle me, bitch, no, you didn't", "And paper, I been sippin' silly", "Hearsin', I been lurkin', catch 'em workin', then I pop out with it"], "meaning": "The lyrics “Tried to handle me, bitch, no, you didn’t” suggest that someone attempted to control or confront the speaker, but was unsuccessful.\n\nThe line “And paper, I been sippin' silly” implies that the speaker has been drinking and possibly indulging in substances.\n\nThe lyrics “Hearsin', I been lurkin', catch ‘em workin’, then I pop out with it” indicate that the speaker has been gathering information or observing others, and when the opportunity arises, they confront or take action.", "annotation_id": "28988294", "url": "https://genius.com/28988294", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Choppa Docter", "line": "I ain’t kill that boy, go ask his boys for all that I know I was countin’ up plenty mula like I hit the lotto", "vault_lines": ["I ain't kill that boy, go ask his boys for all that I know", "I was countin' up plenty mula like I hit the lotto"], "meaning": "The lyrics suggest that the speaker is denying responsibility for killing someone and advises others to ask the deceased person’s friends for more information.\n\nThe line “I ain’t kill that boy” implies the speaker’s innocence and a desire to distance themselves from any accusations or involvement in the incident.\n\nThe following line “go ask his boys for all that I know” suggests that the speaker believes the victim’s friends might have more information about what happened.\n\nOverall, these lyrics convey the speaker’s denial of responsibility and their suggestion to seek information from others involved in the situation.", "annotation_id": "28988280", "url": "https://genius.com/28988280", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Choppa Docter", "line": "With that shit that I is, hit inside his head in front his toddler Diddy boppin’, he got fuckin’ problems", "vault_lines": ["With that shit that I is, hit inside his head in front his toddler", "Diddy boppin', he got fuckin' problems"], "meaning": "The lyrics describe a scenario where someone is facing serious consequences for their actions.\n\nThe line suggests that the person involved has a child, which adds to the gravity of the situation.\n\nIt implies that the person’s reckless behavior is causing problems and has caught the attention of others.", "annotation_id": "28988273", "url": "https://genius.com/28988273", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Choppa Docter", "line": "Divin’ and he better not beat me, bet he not gon’ see next season Cajun, I got season in these heaters, this the hottest feastin’ MAC and it’s small, but lethal, and I call this bitch MAC Mini", "vault_lines": ["Divin' and he better not beat me, bet he not gon' see next season", "Cajun, I got season in these heaters, this the hottest feastin'", "MAC and it's small, but lethal, and I call this bitch MAC Mini"], "meaning": "Cajun: A type of cuisine and culture originating from Louisiana, known for its bold and spicy flavors.\n\n“I got season in these heaters, this the hottest feastin'”: The artist is metaphorically comparing his skills or talents to spicing up a meal, emphasizing that he brings intense heat and excitement to his craft.\n\n“MAC and it’s small, but lethal, and I call this bitch MAC Mini”: The artist is referencing a MAC, which is a type of firearm known for its power despite its compact size. This line suggests that he possesses a powerful and formidable presence despite his relatively small stature.", "annotation_id": "28988257", "url": "https://genius.com/28988257", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Choppa Docter", "line": "Come from rags to gas to riches Went from fightin’, then to killin’ Cool like antifreeze, done blew the ceilin’", "vault_lines": ["Come from rags to gas to riches", "Went from fightin', then to killin'", "Cool like antifreeze, done blew the ceilin'"], "meaning": "“Come from rags to gas to riches” refers to a journey of extreme transformation and success.\n\nIt signifies going from a state of poverty or struggle (“rags”) to a state of wealth and abundance (“gas to riches”).\n\nThe lyrics suggest that the person has overcome challenges and obstacles, evolving from a violent or aggressive past (“fightin', then to killin'”) to a more composed and successful state (“cool like antifreeze, done blew the ceilin'”).", "annotation_id": "28988244", "url": "https://genius.com/28988244", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Choppa Docter", "line": "Hold on, Forgiatos, beat her back in, all that I know Hold on, tell the backup pull onside and shoot the side door", "vault_lines": ["Hold on, Forgiatos, beat her back in, all that I know", "Hold on, tell the backup pull onside and shoot the side door"], "meaning": "In the context of the song “Choppa Doctor” by YoungBoy Never Broke Again, the phrase “Hold on, Forgiatos, beat her back in, all that I know” likely refers to the rapper’s affinity for expensive cars (Forgiatos) and his lifestyle of indulgence and excess.\n\nThe line “Hold on, tell the backup pull onside and shoot the side door” suggests a tense situation where the rapper instructs someone to wait and then forcefully enter a location by shooting open a side door, possibly indicating a disregard for rules or a confrontational attitude.\n\nThese lyrics convey a sense of bravado and assertiveness, reflecting the rapper’s street cred and his willingness to take risks in pursuit of success and status.", "annotation_id": "28988078", "url": "https://genius.com/28988078", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Choppa Docter", "line": "Stolen Glocks inside this Jag’, yeah", "vault_lines": ["Stolen Glocks inside this Jag', yeah"], "meaning": "The lyrics in the song “Choppa Doctor” by YoungBoy Never Broke Again reference stolen Glocks being inside a Jaguar car.\n\nThis line suggests that the artist is associating illegal firearms, specifically stolen Glocks, with the luxury vehicle.\n\nThe mention of the stolen Glocks inside the Jag' adds to the gritty and street-oriented themes often found in YoungBoy Never Broke Again’s music.", "annotation_id": "28988072", "url": "https://genius.com/28988072", "state": "pending", "votes": 3, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
