{"type": "song", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Feel Me", "status": "done", "targets": 1, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Youngboy-never-broke-again-feel-me-lyrics", "genius_title": "Feel Me", "genius_id": 11550755, "source": "api"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Feeling Emotions (Emotions)", "status": "done", "targets": 0, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Youngboy-never-broke-again-and-simply-teelee-feeling-emotions-emotions-lyrics", "genius_title": "Feeling Emotions (Emotions)", "genius_id": 14125546, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Fever", "line": "We only spoke once God, he gave me an oath, and I say I’m only your child’s, so please forgive the shit that I done did", "vault_lines": ["We only spoke once", "God, he gave me an oath, and I say I'm only your child's, so please forgive the shit that I done did"], "meaning": "After reflecting on the isolated and brutal nature of his environment, YoungBoy turns completely to his faith for solace. While “child” is grammatically standard, he explicitly adds an ’s' to “child’s.” This unique vocal choice serves as deep spiritual wordplay. By using the possessive “child’s,” he creates a layered meaning that separates the identity of God (the Father) and Jesus (the Child), signaling that he belongs strictly to the lineage and protection of the Holy Spirit.\n\nRecognizing that his lifestyle is incredibly difficult and full of sin, he uses this oath to directly ask for forgiveness (“please forgive the shit that I done did”), showing that while he feels entirely abandoned by an earthly savior on the streets, his ultimate accountability and hope rest with God. – Me & Gemini.", "annotation_id": "40490476", "url": "https://genius.com/40490476", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Fever", "line": "Ain’t no savior, this life we live so hard He killed you in your front yard Your mom’ll be the one to find you, homie (Your head)", "vault_lines": ["Ain't no savior, this life we live so hard", "He killed you in your front yard", "Your mom'll be the one to find you, homie (Your head)"], "meaning": "In this sequence, the lyrics strip away any glamour from the street lifestyle, offering a stark and sobering warning about its consequences. Stating “Ain’t no savior” emphasizes a feeling of absolute isolation, reminding listeners that in the midst of active conflict, there is no one coming to rescue you from danger.\n\nThe lines quickly paint a vivid and tragic picture of the final outcome of this lifestyle: a targeted attack occurring right outside one’s home (“He killed you in your front yard”). By noting that “Your mom’ll be the one to find you,” the lyrics highlight the devastating collateral damage of street violence, shifting the focus from the victim to the intense trauma inflicted on the family members left behind to deal with the aftermath. – Gemini", "annotation_id": "40490468", "url": "https://genius.com/40490468", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Fever", "line": "Unmarked cars passin’ by Jaz house, and ask her, I was stayin’ there She ain’t understand why I had to shake the spot This the same way, Ben, I leave shit dead Meechy ain’t shake the spot, that’s the same place that he banged it at", "vault_lines": ["Unmarked cars passin' by Jaz house, and ask her, I was stayin' there", "She ain't understand why I had to shake the spot", "This the same way, Ben, I leave shit dead", "Meechy ain't shake the spot, that's the same place that he banged it at"], "meaning": "This sequence maps out a high-stakes timeline involving law enforcement surveillance, personal strain, and inner-circle actions. YoungBoy reveals that undercover officers in unmarked vehicles were constantly patrolling past the home of his now-wife, Jazlyn Gaulden, where he was secretly staying to lay low. Because Jazlyn was outside of the street world, she couldn’t understand why he had to suddenly “shake the spot” (flee the location) to avoid drawing the authorities straight to her front door.\n\nYoungBoy then brings his close associate, NBA Ben 10, into the conversation, explaining that the heavy police presence was a direct reaction to a fatal conflict he was involved in. He hints that the authorities were hunting him precisely because he leaves permanent, lethal consequences in his wake.\n\nFinally, he connects this location to his cousin, NBA Meechy Baby. Top contrasts his own forced departure with Meechy’s response to a threat at that exact same place. While YoungBoy had to leave to protect his family and avoid the law, Meechy stood his ground and “banged it” (returned fire or fought back) right there, showcasing the uncompromising, fearless nature of the Never Broke Again circle when cornered. – Gemini & Me", "annotation_id": "40490455", "url": "https://genius.com/40490455", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Fever", "line": "Million dollars, nigga, if you ain’t know just for a murder fare", "vault_lines": ["Million dollars, nigga, if you ain't know just for a murder fare"], "meaning": "A “murder fare” is street slang for a contract killing payout or bounty. Just like a standard financial fare pays for transit, a murder fare is the price paid to send an enemy to their final destination. Putting a “million dollars” on a single fare highlights both the high-profile nature of his targets and the endless financial resources Top has available to fund his conflicts and protect his circle – Gemini", "annotation_id": "40490406", "url": "https://genius.com/40490406", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Fever", "line": "Slimy, cutthroat, ’til my app’, for sure No, I don’t play it fair", "vault_lines": ["Slimy, cutthroat, 'til my app', for sure", "No, I don't play it fair"], "meaning": "In this line, the artist embraces a completely ruthless mentality to survive his environment. “Slimy” and “cutthroat” are street terms used to describe a cold, unpredictable lifestyle where traditional rules of loyalty and fairness are thrown out the window.\n\nThe phrase “‘til my app’” functions as a clever shorthand for “until my Adam’s apple.” This anatomical metaphor signifies being filled to the absolute brim. By stating he is cutthroat up to his throat, he is declaring that this uncompromising, ruthless nature is a core part of his entire being.\n\nBecause he is fully consumed by this survival mindset, he openly admits, “No, I don’t play it fair.” In a hostile environment, sticking to a fair playing field is a liability, so he chooses to abandon conventional ethics entirely to ensure he always comes out on top. – Gemini", "annotation_id": "40490399", "url": "https://genius.com/40490399", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Fever", "line": "Come through every time you need my help, you test my luck, I fail", "vault_lines": ["Come through every time you need my help, you test my luck, I fail"], "meaning": "Here, the lyrics expose a toxic, one-sided relationship where the other person only reaches out when they require assistance (“Come through every time you need my help”). By constantly draining his resources and testing his patience, they push him into volatile or compromising situations—metaphorically “testing his luck.”\n\nAdmitting “I fail” shows a moment of self-awareness. It reveals that despite knowing he is being used, his loyalty or inability to say no ultimately causes him to slip up, cross his own boundaries, or face negative consequences due to their manipulation. – Gemini", "annotation_id": "40490366", "url": "https://genius.com/40490366", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Fever", "line": "Fucked me up, you ain’t really love me, thugged me, girl", "vault_lines": ["Fucked me up, you ain't really love me, thugged me, girl"], "meaning": "It messed up his mental when he seen the girl he loved wasn’t really rocking with him and instead treated him like he didn’t mean anything to her (“thugging him”).", "annotation_id": "40490356", "url": "https://genius.com/40490356", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Fever", "line": "Head bust, colon system, yellow shit out on the ground", "vault_lines": ["Head bust, colon system, yellow shit out on the ground"], "meaning": "This line uses intensely vivid, raw imagery to detail the immediate aftermath of a fatal street shooting. By referencing a “head bust” alongside damage to the “colon system,” the lyrics depict multiple, catastrophic gunshot wounds to both the upper and lower body.\n\nThe phrase “yellow shit out on the ground” serves a dual purpose. On a literal level, it describes internal bodily fluids and biological matter exposed on the pavement from a severe torso injury. On a symbolic level, it simultaneously mocks the bright yellow police caution tape that law enforcement leaves behind to mark off a crime scene after a successful hit. – Gemini", "annotation_id": "40490345", "url": "https://genius.com/40490345", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Fever", "line": "His partner left him, they couldn’t even pick him up, bitch", "vault_lines": ["His partner left him, they couldn't even pick him up, bitch"], "meaning": "The friend of the dude dead couldn’t even be bothered to pick up his partner, or was likely too scared after seeing his close friend die right in front of his eyes.", "annotation_id": "40490341", "url": "https://genius.com/40490341", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Fever", "line": "When I needed you, you weren’t nowhere to be found Needed you to come through and you had to let me down", "vault_lines": ["Needed you to come through and you had to let me down"], "meaning": "YoungBoy addresses a specific individual, expressing the deep pain of abandonment. During a severe depressive state, he attempted to reach out for support, but his cries for help were met with complete silence. This lack of support left him feeling astray, down, and entirely hopeless. However, despite being let down, he ultimately prevailed over the despair and found the strength to overcome it through his faith in God.", "annotation_id": "40490319", "url": "https://genius.com/40490319", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Fever", "line": "I sip drank, for real Now, I don’t really know ’bout that dude But, all I know he asked to buy a loan and that shit ain’t cool", "vault_lines": ["I sip drank, for real", "Now, I don't really know 'bout that dude"], "meaning": "“Drank” (lean/codeine cough syrup) is a highly expensive and tightly controlled substance in street circles. In this line, YoungBoy addresses someone trying to acquire it from him under shady terms.\n\nAsking to “buy a loan” is an oxymoron and a major red flag in street economics. It implies the person wants the expensive substance upfront with a vague promise to pay it back later, essentially treating a high-value street commodity like a bank loan.\n\nThis is considered a complete violation of etiquette because it forces the provider to take on all the financial risk for someone who likely doesn’t have the means to pay. By shutting this down, Top is highlighting that he doesn’t tolerate bad business, manipulation, or people trying to take advantage of his resources. – Gemini", "annotation_id": "40490314", "url": "https://genius.com/40490314", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Fever", "line": "Look, look, I protect my life from all this shit that I done been through (Talk to ’em, Top) I’ve been poppin’ pills ‘cause, bruh, this shit been helpin’ me get through (Talk to ’em, Top)", "vault_lines": ["Look, look, I protect my life from all this shit that I done been through (Talk to 'em, Top)", "I've been poppin' pills 'cause, bruh, this shit been helpin' me get through (Talk to 'em, Top)"], "meaning": "YoungBoy stating “I protect my life from all this shit that I done been through” emphasizes that his history has forced him into a constant state of high alert and survival mode.\n\nHe immediately follows this by admitting to popping pills just to help him “get through.” This creates a clear link between his past trauma and his reliance on self-medication to cope with day-to-day life.\n\nThe internal ad-libs (“Talk to ‘em, Top”) serve to reinforce these main lines, showing him pushing himself to be honest about this heavy cycle of survival and coping. – Gemini", "annotation_id": "40490297", "url": "https://genius.com/40490297", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Fever", "line": "Every night on the phone with them hitters and I’m tryna make sure they make it home through the night", "vault_lines": ["Every night on the phone with them hitters and I'm tryna make sure they make it home through the night"], "meaning": "YoungBoy reveals that he conducts nightly check-ins with his associates (“hitters”). Rather than just discussing street business, these phone calls are driven by a genuine concern for their survival, as he actively monitors their safety to ensure they make it home securely and haven’t fallen victim to retaliatory violence. – Gemini", "annotation_id": "40490252", "url": "https://genius.com/40490252", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
