{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Fresh Prince Of Utah", "line": "This is a story all about how A pistol totin’ kid named YoungBoy grew up in the Nawf Whole life in war, he got some money then he moved to Utah But still ain’t stop from the top the mountain, he steppin’, knockin’ shit off, bitch Still with the clique, you know for shit, we don’t switch", "vault_lines": ["This is a story all about how", "A pistol totin' kid named YoungBoy grew up in the Nawf", "Whole life in war, he got some money then he moved to Utah", "But still ain't stop from the top the mountain, he steppin', knockin' shit off, bitch", "Still with the clique, you know for shit, we don't switch"], "meaning": "Interpolating the lyrics from the theme song of The Fresh Prince of Bel-Air — a 90s comedy starring Will Smith — YoungBoy tweaks the lyrics to fit his own adolescence. In the original version, it’s noted that Will grows up in “West Philidelphia,” however YoungBoy notes that he grew up in the “Nawf” — referring to the Northside of Baton Rouge, Louisiana.\n\nNow this is a story all about how\nMy life got flipped turned upside down\nAnd I’d like to take a minute, just sit right there\nI’ll tell you how I became the prince of a town called Bel-Air\nIn West Philadelphia born and raised\nOn the playground is where I spent most of my days\nChillin' out, maxin', relaxin' all cool\nAnd all shootin' some b-ball outside of the school\n\nYoungBoy describes himself as a “pistol totin' kid” as he adds context to the upcoming lyrics where he states that his “whole life [has been a] war.” This could be a particular connection to the gang life YoungBoy has been surrounded by in his teenage years, being arrested a multitude of times from minor drug and robbery charges to kidnapping and attempted murder.\n\nFlowing at a fast pace, YoungBoy continues to go through his life story as he raps that after getting money, he moved to Utah. On October 26, 2021, YoungBoy was released from jail on a $1.5 million bond and has been on house arrest in Salt Lake City, Utah since. Relocated in Salt Lake City, YoungBoy purchased a multi-million dollar mansion atop a mountain looking down at the Great Salt Lake — the home has most notably been seen in the music videos of “Vette Motors” and “Put It On Me.”\n\nIn the midst of switching his flow, YoungBoy reaffirms his affiliation with the Bloods as he has in previous songs, most notably “I Hate YoungBoy” in which he rapped:\n\nNigga, I’m a real Blood (Blood), and I’m screamin', “Blatt” (Blatt)", "annotation_id": "27916377", "url": "https://genius.com/27916377", "state": "accepted", "votes": 22, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
