{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Fuck The Industry Pt. 2", "line": "Bitch, you ain’t heard? Back from Baton Rouge to that ’Nolia, we cutthroat", "vault_lines": ["Bitch, you ain't heard? Back from Baton Rouge to that 'Nolia, we cutthroat"], "meaning": "Youngboy comes from Baton Rouge and Nolia means New Orlean. Both cities are in Louisiana and are close", "annotation_id": "38098781", "url": "https://genius.com/38098781", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Fuck The Industry Pt. 2", "line": "I paid them young niggas, word ‘round, they claimin’ they gon’ kill me", "vault_lines": ["Don't forget it, I paid them young niggas, word 'round, they claimin' they gon' kill me"], "meaning": "YB is talking about how he paid his gang members to split niggas since word around town was niggas were trying to get at him to kill him", "annotation_id": "35785108", "url": "https://genius.com/35785108", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Fuck The Industry Pt. 2", "line": "That hitter came way from New Orleans, that split him in them apartments So Mr. Rap Boy, shit like that what got me as a target Don’t forget it", "vault_lines": ["That hitter came way from New Orleans, that split him in them apartments", "So Mr. Rap Boy, shit like that what got me as a target"], "meaning": "The hitter that came from New Orleans was from YB camp and he split Blvd Quick at his apartment complex for dissing YB, his mama, and his whole crew. So, YB sent a hitter to get rid of Blvd Quick", "annotation_id": "35785061", "url": "https://genius.com/35785061", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Fuck The Industry Pt. 2", "line": "Top say, “Fuck these niggas all in the industry” Bitch-ass niggas be cliquin’ up and thinkin’ they gon’ finish me Bitch, I’ll take these millions to get at you, it ain’t no gettin’ at me Bitch, I’ll send them hitters to hit at you, boy, don’t say shit to me", "vault_lines": ["Bitch, I'll send them hitters to hit at you, boy, don't say shit to me"], "meaning": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again seemingly depicts the tragic September 2017 incident where YoungBoy’s ex-associate, NBA Pap, was indicted on the death of YoungBoy’s biggest rival, Gee Money—who both this song’s name and lyrics diss, as he has a diss track dedicated to YoungBoy titled “Industry.”\n\nYoungBoy’s and Gee’s beef started after YoungBoy blew up and stopped hanging around him. However, by August 2017, Gee had already dedicated his diss track “Industry” to YoungBoy—where he also spoke disrespectfully on YoungBoy’s sister and spoke on him and his gang’s desire of wanting to shoot YoungBoy dead.\n\nIn a 2017 interview with SAY CHEESE!, Gee expanded on YoungBoy’s switch up after his rise-to-fame, stating:\n\n[YoungBoy’s also] mad about his sister too, though. I fucked her a long time ago. He wasn’t really trippin' on it back then, though, you know what I’m sayin'? He [referred to me as] “Big brother” and shit, though, ya heard me? He just lettin' that famous shit get to his head. Now, he’s feelin' like he’s this new nigga.\n\nCoincidentally or not, Gee’s best friend, Fredo Bang, has a collaborative remix song with Lil Durk titled “Top (Remix).”", "annotation_id": "30598958", "url": "https://genius.com/30598958", "state": "accepted", "votes": 19, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Fuck The Industry Pt. 2", "line": "I done did more time than more time you niggas spent inside the trenches", "vault_lines": ["I done did more time than more time you niggas spent inside the trenches"], "meaning": "Here, YoungBoy is saying that he has spent more time in jail than his opps have spent insine the trenches.", "annotation_id": "28689918", "url": "https://genius.com/28689918", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Fuck The Industry Pt. 2", "line": "I got Stunna with me and, bitch, I got Fee, that’s to get money with me", "vault_lines": ["I got Stunna with me and, bitch, I got Fee, that's to get money with me"], "meaning": "Stunna is a widely known nickname for the Cash Money Records CEO and founder, Birdman who is known to have had a close relationship with YoungBoy since the start of his career — releasing several tracks together alongside a collaborative mixtape, From The Bayou. Fee Banks — YoungBoy’s previous manager  — is the CEO of the management company and label, Good Money Global.\n\nBeing cosigned by Birdman and Fee early in his career, since the release of his debut studio album, Until Death Calls My Name where both executives appeared in several music videos, has seen to help YoungBoy throughout his career. Birdman spoke about YoungBoy in a 2020 interview in which he noted:\n\nWatching how fast he do music and the value of the music, I saw a lot of similarities between him and Wayne. I seen stardom in him, but I knew it was a process.I once was somebody like him and had to gamble my life. I wanted to show him that he could really survive off his talent. You could go to jail, or you could die, or you could try to be somebody.\n\nFurthermore, YoungBoy’s mentioning of getting money could be a reference to Birdman’s Cash Money Records and Lil Wayne’s Young Money Entertainment.", "annotation_id": "28594099", "url": "https://genius.com/28594099", "state": "accepted", "votes": 20, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Fuck The Industry Pt. 2", "line": "And I know when the rain pour, it could be me or either of them that’s gone", "vault_lines": ["And I know when the rain pour, it could be me or either of them that's gone"], "meaning": "In this line our majesty, Lil Top, is comparing the firing of semi automatic weaponry to that of pouring rain. Hence “when the rain pours” it could be him and his affiliates, or the opposition that are killed during the altercation.", "annotation_id": "28524176", "url": "https://genius.com/28524176", "state": "pending", "votes": 10, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Fuck The Industry Pt. 2", "line": "Hold on, so long they’ve been tryna X me", "vault_lines": ["Hold on, so long they've been tryna X me"], "meaning": "YB refers to group/s of people trying to x him out or take/cross him out. This can either be interpreted as some people are trying to kill YB or blackball him in the industry.", "annotation_id": "28522425", "url": "https://genius.com/28522425", "state": "pending", "votes": 5, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Fuck The Industry Pt. 2", "line": "Play with me, Lil Yachty mad about JT, faggot", "vault_lines": ["Play with me, Lil Yachty mad about JT, faggot"], "meaning": "Lil Yachty’s response:\n\nhttps://twitter.com/lilyachty/status/1656861273852899329?s=20\n\nYoungBoy’s supposed diss towards Lil Yachty could be in response to this video of Lil Yachty imitating/mocking YoungBoy.\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/shorts/2LGpnXZzuI0", "annotation_id": "28511762", "url": "https://genius.com/28511762", "state": "pending", "votes": 44, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Fuck The Industry Pt. 2", "line": "Talked to Drake ‘cross FaceTime, he wasn’t feelin’ me Told me that he fuck with Durk, damn, that shit gettin’ to me Told me that he like the shit I’m doin’, but can’t do shit with me So when we cross our ways, fuck what you say, bitch, you my enemy", "vault_lines": ["Talked to Drake 'cross FaceTime, he wasn't feelin' me", "Told me that he fuck with Durk, damn, that shit gettin' to me", "Told me that he like the shit I'm doin', but can't do shit with me", "So when we cross our ways, fuck what you say, bitch, you my enemy"], "meaning": "YoungBoy disses rapper Chicago native Lil Durk, who played a big role in the mixtape’s promo as it was originally announced in a—now-deleted—diss-tweet towards Durk. YoungBoy and Durk’s beef already resulted in a number of shots sent toward each other through music, which can be found throughout Durk’s and YoungBoy’s February 2022 tracks “AHHH HA” and “I Hate YoungBoy.”\n\nIt’s seen that YoungBoy boldly sends shots toward Canadian rapper Drake for accompanying a friendship with Durk after making it seem as though he was supporting him. A collaboration between YB and Drake was officially announced by YoungBoy’s brother OG 3Three in 2021, but it was unconfirmed whether it would be finished or released.\n\nHowever, Drake and Durk have collaborated before on their August 2020 track “Laugh Now Cry Later” and September 2021 “In The Bible.”\n\nOn May 18, 2023, J Prince, the man who introduced Drake to Lil Wayne’s Young Money, resulting in Drake getting signed and making his way into the rap industry shared a post with him, Birdman, and YoungBoy in which he alluded that the two artists had squashed their differences:\n\n\nI must address the song where my name and drakes name were mentioned.\n\nTo the lil homie, as I said to you privately, I say to you publicly that Drake is my son, he roll with me. Therefore, I think it’s a good idea to take him off your enemy list because the truth of the matter is that we got nothing but love for Durk and your accomplishments.\n\nWhen I put Drake on that facetime call with you, my intent was for y’all to move forward, not backwards. Two things we get every day is a chance and a choice. The choices we make determine our destination.", "annotation_id": "28511438", "url": "https://genius.com/28511438", "state": "accepted", "votes": 57, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Fuck The Industry Pt. 2", "line": "J a ho, that nigga played it cold like he was gon’ do a feature So I texted his line a muscle sign, I swear it’s gon’ be nice to meet you", "vault_lines": ["J a ho, that nigga played it cold like he was gon' do a feature", "So I texted his line a muscle sign, I swear it's gon' be nice to meet you"], "meaning": "YoungBoy sends a subliminal shot at North Carolina rapper J. Cole.\n\nIn March of 2021, it was revealed that YoungBoy had a studio session planned with Cole in which he made Cole wait for eight hours and never showed up.\n\nAfter receiving backlash and being asked in an interview about the situation, YB said:\n\nI was going through something, so what the fuck I'ma go in this man’s studio session for a meet up with him, knowing that I ain’t in a mode to talk or just to be in the mode to fuck around? What the fuck I'ma go around this man if I know I ain’t in a mood to talk to nobody or sit there, or even be around somebody?\n\nWith this line, YoungBoy does not only reaffirm that he is not at blame for the situation, but instead blames Cole for never getting back in touch with him, and sending him a threat for if they ever meet.\n\n“Played it cold” is a play on the rapper’s name.", "annotation_id": "28511386", "url": "https://genius.com/28511386", "state": "accepted", "votes": 48, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
