{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "N.B.A.", "line": "Mind of a Menace had the whole world shook", "vault_lines": ["Mind of a Menace had the whole world shook"], "meaning": "This is a direct reference to YoungBoy’s debut mixtape, Mind of a Menace, released in 2015. With this line, he claims that his arrival on the music scene was so powerful and aggressive that it had the “whole world shook,” establishing the high-energy and rebellious persona that would eventually propel him to superstardom.", "annotation_id": "38178193", "url": "https://genius.com/38178193", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "N.B.A.", "line": "I want fifty grand in my pants like I’m Boosie", "vault_lines": ["I want fifty grand in my pants like I'm Boosie"], "meaning": "YoungBoy pays homage to Baton Rouge pioneer Lil Boosie by referencing his iconic line from the 2007 hit “Wipe Me Down.” By stating he wants “fifty grand in [his] pants,” YoungBoy is manifesting the same legendary status and financial independence that Boosie achieved, aligning himself with the lineage of Louisiana rap royalty.", "annotation_id": "38178185", "url": "https://genius.com/38178185", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "N.B.A.", "line": "And get back to the hood, like I’m Frank Lucas (Like, like I’m Frank Lucas)", "vault_lines": ["And get back to the hood, like I'm Frank Lucas (Like, like I'm Frank Lucas)"], "meaning": "YoungBoy compares his return to his neighborhood to that of the infamous Harlem kingpin Frank Lucas. Just as Lucas (portrayed by Denzel Washington in American Gangster) was known for returning to his community with immense wealth and “giving back” to maintain his status and support his people, YoungBoy signals that his success hasn’t detached him from his roots—it has only given him more power within them.", "annotation_id": "38178178", "url": "https://genius.com/38178178", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "N.B.A.", "line": "Listen to Scooter cause I keep them shooters where I show (Shooters with me)", "vault_lines": ["Listen to Scooter cause I keep them shooters where I show (Shooters with me)"], "meaning": "YoungBoy pays homage to Atlanta rapper Young Scooter, suggesting that his music serves as a soundtrack to his own reality. The line serves as a warning: much like the themes in Scooter’s music, YoungBoy maintains a heavy security detail of “shooters” who accompany him to every public appearance or “show” to ensure his safety.", "annotation_id": "38178161", "url": "https://genius.com/38178161", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "N.B.A.", "line": "Looked up to French Montana ’cause I wanted dope", "vault_lines": ["Looked up to French Montana 'cause I wanted dope"], "meaning": "YoungBoy reflects on his early influences and his mindset before his rap career took off. By citing French Montana—who famously rose to fame after his “Cocaine City” era—YoungBoy highlights how he initially viewed the drug trade or the “hustler” lifestyle as his primary path to wealth and success.", "annotation_id": "38178155", "url": "https://genius.com/38178155", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "N.B.A.", "line": "Pull on side me like I’m Biggie, send ’em through the car door (Bap, bap-bap-bap-bap)", "vault_lines": ["Pull on side me like I'm Biggie, send 'em through the car door (Bap, bap-bap-bap-bap)"], "meaning": "He is suggesting that if someone approaches his vehicle with hostile intent (imitating the way Biggie was approached), he is prepared to engage in a shootout.", "annotation_id": "38178146", "url": "https://genius.com/38178146", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "N.B.A.", "line": "In my hood used to want to be Nino Put my headphones in, bumping Ochocinco", "vault_lines": ["In my hood used to want to be Nino"], "meaning": "YoungBoy contrasts his street ambitions with his musical influences. He identifies with the “hustler” archetype of Nino Brown from New Jack City while highlighting French Montana’s “Ochocinco” as the soundtrack to his youth, illustrating the culture that shaped his mindset before his own rise to fame.", "annotation_id": "38178133", "url": "https://genius.com/38178133", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
