{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Now Who", "line": "Fefeezy", "vault_lines": ["I got good money 'round the globe, I did my shit like Fefeezy"], "meaning": "He talking bout Fee Banks his old manager, Fee got alot of money and shyt like that", "annotation_id": "30400110", "url": "https://genius.com/30400110", "state": "pending", "votes": 2, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Now Who", "line": "I ain’t lose my brain, come with my change, fuck all this fame, my name been blemished", "vault_lines": ["I ain't lose my brain, comе with my change, fuck all this fame, my name been blemished"], "meaning": "Following YoungBoy’s transition to Motown Records, his style of music has been noticed to have changed as he’s stopped rapping as aggressively as he once was, and his music is a lot more dancey and fun instead of violent as what his fans tend to appreciate more.\n\nDuring his February 2023 interview with Billboard, YoungBoy expressed that when looking back at how his music influenced the people around him and the younger generation to act out and think it was cool to get arrested, he decided that it was time for him to switch up the lyrical content and subject matter within his music. Nearing the end of the interview, he stated that he refuses to go back to his old ways:\n\nYou can’t be on top forever, you know? Because I’m not changing. I will not be provoked, I will not be broken, and I’m not going back to who I used to be. Accept it or not — I ain’t going back. I’m only going to get more groovy from here.\n\nFollowing this, YoungBoy was heavily criticized by fans after releasing tracks with high-pitched vocals and participating in dances that affected his “gangster” image.\n\nIn YoungBoy’s July 2022 track “I Don’t Talk,” he rapped a similar lyric as he referred to him being blackballed by his ex-label:\n\nThey talk down on my name, on my face, they brought shame\nBlackballed me from up out the game, bitch, stripped me out the fame", "annotation_id": "29990263", "url": "https://genius.com/29990263", "state": "accepted", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Now Who", "line": "Fuck up my deal, get put in a pickle, nigga", "vault_lines": ["Fuck up my deal, get put in a pickle, nigga, think it's a game, die whеn we scrimmage"], "meaning": "YoungBoy’s battle with his ex-label Atlantic Records has been extremely public over the past few years with YoungBoy claiming they were attempting to blackball him from the music industry while pleading for upcoming artists to not sign with them.\n\nOn May 17, 2022, just months prior to The Last Slimeto, YoungBoy’s fifth studio album which would finalize his departure from the label, it was announced that the rapper turned down a $25 million record deal to re-sign with the label, expressing to DJ Akademiks about the situation:\n\nThey ain’t gonna come back to me because I ain’t trying to hear s**t they got to say\n\nIt’s worth mentioning that YoungBoy publically called out the label in February 2022 following his mixtape Colors being taken down from the Apple Music albums charts, claiming that this was another attempt to blackball him:\n\nI was going #1 two weeks straight with a mixtape so they took it down off the charts.\n[…]\nI don’t give a f**k you still can’t stop me don’t sign to Atlantic if you an artist they not gone support you especially if you live a certain way.\n[…]\nWhy blackball me, I’m the good guy!!??\n[…]\nDon’t Sign To Atlantic Unless You Want To Be A Slave\n\nIn YoungBoy’s most recent interview with the Rap Radar podcast following his departure from Atlantic and his signing with Motown, he slyly referred to why he wouldn’t re-sign with Atlantic:\n\nNo matter if I got $2, it’s actually somebody makin' $4 off of my creation. So until I get the business down pack— Like, to where I go— Like, I done built my own team, like, I don’t need no label, like, like. This whole system is what I built and this all I need to get everything I need to get everything I want from out of it.", "annotation_id": "29990167", "url": "https://genius.com/29990167", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Now Who", "line": "I tote that chopper when I roll in the NO like BGeezy", "vault_lines": ["I tote that chopper when I roll in the NO like BGeezy"], "meaning": "B.G. is an American rapper who was also signed to Cash Money Records alongside Lil Wayne whom YoungBoy referred to earlier in the track. G.G. was also a fourth of the Hot Boys alongside Wayne, Turk, and Juvenile.\n\n“N-O” is an abbreviation for the city of New Orleans, Louisiana, the city which B.G. hails from. YoungBoy being from Baton Rouge, Louisiana, just over an hour and a half away from the aforementioned city has several major connections with the city as he’s affiliated with Birdman’s Cash Money Records and is managed by Fee Banks who’s also Lil Wayne’s manager.\n\nIt’s worth mentioning that B.G. was recently released from a fourteen-year prison sentence—which he served eleven years—regarding a Federal firearm charge, hence why YoungBoy likens himself to the rapper due to his current situation where he’s also fighting a similar case. In the lyric, YB compares himself to the artist as he notes that when he’s in New Orleans, he always has a firearm with him to protect himself.", "annotation_id": "29989962", "url": "https://genius.com/29989962", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Now Who", "line": "Straight out that boot like I’m Weezy", "vault_lines": ["Can't stay off boot while in that booth, straight out that boot like I'm Weezy"], "meaning": "YoungBoy was, much like Lil Wayne, born and raised in Louisiana. On a map, the state is roughly shaped like a boot.", "annotation_id": "29919194", "url": "https://genius.com/29919194", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
