{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "The Gaulden’s", "line": "Told my ho, “You tryna make me, or break?”", "vault_lines": ["Told my ho, \"You tryna make me, or break?\" Yeah"], "meaning": "Reference to his old song “Break Or Make Me,” basically asking his wife is she tryna break him down, break his heart, make him feel like nothing. Or was she tryna make him out of something, help boost his career and lifestyle, and more. Make him a better man than before.", "annotation_id": "40646265", "url": "https://genius.com/40646265", "state": "pending", "votes": 0, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "The Gaulden’s", "line": "I’m known for blowin’ up these niggas block, and makin’ them drop like songs (What else?)", "vault_lines": ["I'm known for blowin' up these niggas blocks, and makin' 'em drop like songs (What else?)"], "meaning": "YB is comparing the way he shoots up rivalry blocks to “blowing up blocks” making it seem like he’s comparing his weaponry to the act of bombing a neighborhood block. “Making ‘em drop like songs” is also YoungBoy comparing the way bodies drop after being shot up to the way one drops their own songs.", "annotation_id": "40646176", "url": "https://genius.com/40646176", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "The Gaulden’s", "line": "When I pull up, they runnin’ and jumpin’ them fences, I’m throwin’ bombs like Sudan", "vault_lines": ["When I pull up, they runnin' and jumpin' them fences, I'm throwin' bombs like Sudan"], "meaning": "The first half of the bar highlights the imagery of enemies “runnin' and jumpin' them fences” when he arrives demonstrates immediate fear. The “Bombs” Metaphor is YoungBoy comparing the firepower of his weaponry directly to explosive devices. He is not using football slang here; rather, the phrase “throwin' bombs” highlights the sound of his firearms. The gunshots are so incredibly loud, destructive, and high-caliber that they mimic the auditory impact and chaos of actual bombs detonating during an attack.\n\nThe Sudan Reference: The line culminates in a political reference to Sudan, a country that has endured devastating military conflicts and heavy bombardment. By comparing his actions to Sudan, YoungBoy draws a parallel between his localized street conflicts and actual warfare. It emphasizes that the explosive, deafening noise of his weapons turns his block into a high-intensity combat zone. Mostly me, last part is Gemini AI.", "annotation_id": "40646081", "url": "https://genius.com/40646081", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "The Gaulden’s", "line": "I had granted Mellow wishes, this bitch gon’ play me like I was a pawn", "vault_lines": ["I had granted Mellow wishes, this bitch gon' play me like I was a pawn"], "meaning": "YoungBoy expresses a deep sense of betrayal by a woman he provided for, feeling his generosity was met with manipulation and disloyalty. He compares this, where he feels used like an expendable chess piece, to his former relationship, which he claims he was generous in. – Gemini AI", "annotation_id": "40646060", "url": "https://genius.com/40646060", "state": "pending", "votes": -1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
