{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Villain", "line": "(YoungBoy who you talkin’ ’bout?)", "vault_lines": [], "meaning": "Youngboy in this lyric, is asking himself, who is he talking about. The following lyric then answers, he no longer will speak with who he is presumably talking to.", "annotation_id": "25313324", "url": "https://genius.com/25313324", "state": "pending", "votes": 1, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Villain", "line": "NBA, double G, I bleed that", "vault_lines": ["NBA, double G, I bleed that (We don't hide)"], "meaning": "Gucci is a luxury fashion brand formed in 1921. The brand is referenced countless times in hip-hop, and is is essential for flexing, or showing off your expensive items.\n\nGucci’s logo (shown below) consists of two intertwined G’s, known as “the double G’s.”\nYoungboy is stating that he wears so much Gucci, he practically bleeds it.", "annotation_id": "14476222", "url": "https://genius.com/14476222", "state": "accepted", "votes": -6, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Villain", "line": "400 Degreez like Juvenile", "vault_lines": ["400 Degreez like Juvenile"], "meaning": "Youngboy is referencing the rapper Juvenile (shown below), who is a Louisiana native like Youngboy himself and has collaborated with some of Louisiana’s most notable rappers like Lil Wayne.\n\n\n\nIn 1998, Juvenile dropped his breakout project, 400 Degreez which contained his biggest hit, “Back That Azz Up”.", "annotation_id": "14465448", "url": "https://genius.com/14465448", "state": "accepted", "votes": 9, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Villain", "line": "Chief Keef, we be drillin’ shit", "vault_lines": ["Chief Keef, we be drillin' shit"], "meaning": "Chief Keef is a rapper from Chicago, Illinois. He is often said to be the one to popularize drill music.\n\nHis earlier songs like Love Sosa, I Don’t Like, and Love No Thotties are all songs that, not only started his career, but also made drill music a lot more popular.", "annotation_id": "14436478", "url": "https://genius.com/14436478", "state": "accepted", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "song", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "Villain", "status": "done", "targets": 4, "clicked": 0, "genius_url": "https://genius.com/Youngboy-never-broke-again-villain-lyrics", "genius_title": "Villain", "genius_id": 3675707, "source": "api"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "WTF", "line": "They want action, tell my niggas, “Cause a scene”", "vault_lines": ["They want action, tell my niggas, \"Cause a scene\""], "meaning": "Nicki says that when her opps want action, she has her people cause a scene.\nHowever, this also relates to the fact that when someone shoots a movie scene, they say words like ‘action’ before starting to shoot", "annotation_id": "28336065", "url": "https://genius.com/28336065", "state": "pending", "votes": 7, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "WTF", "line": "Thinkin’ ‘bout my lil’ brother Kendell, I start ventin’", "vault_lines": ["Thinkin' 'bout my lil' brother Kendell, I start ventin'"], "meaning": "Kendell Gaulden, also known as NBA Ken, is NBA YoungBoy’s younger brother. Kendell earned his notability via an Instagram live with YoungBoy.\n\nHe also appeared in YoungBoy’s previous discography such as his August 2017 hit “Wat Chu Gone Do,” where he was seen sitting at the beginning of the music video.\n\n\n\nPictured: Ken sat outside a disclosed house.\n\nYoungBoy exposes the clear motive for his little brother’s safety as he seeks worrisome in his brother, considering he’s open to getting involved in gang-related violence.\n\nYoungBoy has promoted stopping the violence on the streets in his December 2022 track “This Not a Song ‘This For My Supporters,’” he claims:\n\nYoungin tryna put that blick on somethin' (Baow)\nYoungin steady tryna hit at somethin' (Boom, baow)\nI been tryna tell him not to do it, youngin\nAnd these pussy niggas gon' tell up on me\nI feel your pain, my slime\nJust trust me and I could get you through the rain, my five", "annotation_id": "28250433", "url": "https://genius.com/28250433", "state": "accepted", "votes": 38, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "WTF", "line": "Most these niggas claim 4KTrey ain’t even fuckin’ with me Most these niggas gon’ die today, if they try, know they can’t get me", "vault_lines": ["Most these niggas gon' die today, if they try, know they can't get me"], "meaning": "“4KTrey” or “4KT” is an abbreviation created by YoungBoy Never Broke Again which stands for “Forever Killing Them,” or “Four Killer Team.” 4KTrey is commonly treated as a gang or a group by YoungBoy and its affiliates. The color green is typically associated with the group, hence the Never Broke Again compilation project being titled Green Flag Activity — an evident reference to the gang. In April 2022, YoungBoy also released a track titled “4KT Baby” in which the artist raps about the gang’s doings.\n\nYoungBoy notes that despite people saying they are a member of 4KTrey, they have a revulsion towards YoungBoy, the group’s founder. This can be seen as a reference to NoCap, a former Never Broke Again signee who has rapped about the gang in several of his songs. In late 2022, YoungBoy and NoCap exchanged heated shots through social media in which YoungBoy stated that NoCap was allegedly disloyal towards him and that refused to stand by Quando Rondo following his part in the murder of King Von:\n\nYou ran with me because I was popping at the moment. Shit hit the fan you couldn’t even take a picture with Quando. You a bitch. Fuck you, n***a. Let yo mama love you. Go make a song bout it bitch, make me some money. The show must go on.\n\nFurthermore, YoungBoy rapped a similar lyric in his February 2022 diss track, “I Hate YoungBoy” following the  mention of 4KTrey:\n\nHah, yeah, it’s 4KTrey, Nawfside, 38, nigga, free DDawg\n(Fuck the whole industry, they know, don’t try to get at him)\nWe put guns to the face, bitch-ass nigga, how you wan' do it?\nYou already know what I say, nigga, who gon' die today?", "annotation_id": "28226253", "url": "https://genius.com/28226253", "state": "accepted", "votes": 32, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "WTF", "line": "Who the fuck these niggas is? Who the fuck these bitches? Huh, who the fuck these people are? Tell ’em that we can’t kick it", "vault_lines": ["Who the fuck these people are?", "Who the fuck these bitches?", "Huh, who the fuck these people are?", "Tell 'em that we can't kick it"], "meaning": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again and Nicki Minaj use a rhetorical question to make it clear that they do not get along with the artists and executives in the music industry and are thought to be seen as argumentative and belligerent to other artists and executives in the rap industry.\n\nIn early 2022, following the death of King Von, YoungBoy and Lil Durk were seen as the faces of a large internet controversy in which some of the most successful artists in the industry such as Lil Baby, DaBaby, 21 Savage, and NLE Choppa were forced to choose sides, risking future collaborations with the artists where the majority sided with Durk. Subsequently, YoungBoy claimed Atlantic Records — his previous label — attempted to blackball the artist:\n\nI was going #1 two weeks straight with a mixtape so they took it down off the charts.\n[…]\nI don’t give a f**k you still can’t stop me don’t sign to Atlantic if you an artist they not gone support you especially if you live a certain way.\n[…]\nWhy blackball me, I’m the good guy!!??\n\nSimilarly, Nicki Minaj also faces major controversy against other female artists in the music industry such as Cardi B, Latto, and Miley Cyrus. In 2022, following “Blick Blick” — her viral collaboration with Coi Leray — Nicki spoke about the hate she receives from artists in the industry, their fans, and music executives:\n\nY’all have never experienced hate the way I have experienced. Y’all have just experienced some of the growing pains of artistry, and of social media. Y’all be thinking y’all have experienced hate and trust me, you ain’t experienced it yet… people have been gentle.\n\nThe chorus being shared between both artists can play into the fact that their positions in the industry are similar — Nicki and YoungBoy, despite having some of the largest streaming and sales numbers in comparison to other artists in the industry, both receive a large amount of driven hate towards them and their fan bases.", "annotation_id": "28226128", "url": "https://genius.com/28226128", "state": "accepted", "votes": 46, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "WTF", "line": "Fully active, I keep my shooters in the gym, nigga, practice (Practice) You bitches know it could get sticky like a cactus (Cactus)", "vault_lines": ["You bitches know it could get sticky like a cactus (Cactus)"], "meaning": "Nicki uses a play on words for “active shooter”.\n\nAlso, Nicki is having her shooters ready by having them practice in the Gym.\nWhen someone works out, they sweat a lot. That is why the gym is such a sticky and sweaty place.\n\nIn the next line, however, Nicki uses the alternate meaning of stick. Here, it refers to the sharp sticks all over a cactus.", "annotation_id": "28218805", "url": "https://genius.com/28218805", "state": "pending", "votes": 12, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "WTF", "line": "And still can’t catch a break like fractures (Like fractures)", "vault_lines": ["And still can't catch a break like fractures (Like fractures)"], "meaning": "The wordplay here is with the 3 meanings of the homophones ‘break/break/brake’\n\n1.) Despite treading on Nicki (as mentioned in the previous line), female rappers are still unable to capitalise on that. In the end, Nicki breaks records and remains on Billboard charts while the ones treading on her, do not.\n\n\n\n2.) When female rappers are literally treading on Nicki with tractors, they can’t seem to catch a break. Here, break would rather mean a pause from treading on Nicki. Due to them not stopping, Nicki might physically fracture herself.\n\n\n\n3.) Nicki also uses ‘brake’ as in the brakes of the tractor, which means that the rappers treading on her are not stopping the tractor at all.\n\nNicki also plays with the words ‘fractures’ and ‘break’ , as fracture literally means break(ing of bones)", "annotation_id": "28218743", "url": "https://genius.com/28218743", "state": "pending", "votes": 16, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "WTF", "line": "I’m the one that they tread like tractors", "vault_lines": ["I'm the one that they tread like tractors"], "meaning": "After shETHER dropped, the Nicki Minaj hate train ran everywhere and everyone felt comfortable to disrespect on Nicki. So, in the end, despite the loads of female rappers, she is the one who is to be tread on and treated poorly.\n\nAlso, ‘tread’ here sounds like ‘trail’. In addition to the hate train, she refers to how she is such a trendsetter and trail blazer that she has people following right behind her (like a tractor trailer) & copying her every move. It’s also why she loves to say that all these b***hes are her sons.", "annotation_id": "28218742", "url": "https://genius.com/28218742", "state": "pending", "votes": 14, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "weak"}
{"type": "line", "artist": "YoungBoy Never Broke Again", "song": "WTF", "line": "Cross YoungBoy, then you cross the Queen (Queen)", "vault_lines": ["Cross YoungBoy, then you cross the Queen (Queen)"], "meaning": "Nicki Minaj uses figurative language to mention that if one goes against NBA YoungBoy, then they go against her too, displaying her loyalty to the Baton Rouge native.\n\nOften referred to as “The Queen,” Nicki reiterates her position in the music industry as being the nineteenth highest-selling female artist while being the highest-selling female rapper in the music industry.\n\nThe sense of loyalty between the two artists can be seen as both are viewed as blackballed in the music industry despite their success, power, and influence over the industry — Nicki being the sixth highest-selling rapper of all time and YoungBoy being the second highest streamed rapper in 2022 without support from his previous label.", "annotation_id": "28218185", "url": "https://genius.com/28218185", "state": "accepted", "votes": 17, "second_sense": true, "clicked": false, "click_status": "api", "strength": "accepted"}
